SAP Core Modules Overview

SAP is an Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) system that helps organizations manage different business processes in an integrated way. SAP consists of several core modules, with each module focusing on a specific business function.

Core SAP Modules
Finance — FI
Controlling — CO
Material Management — MM
Sales & Distribution — SD
Production Planning — PP
Human Capital Management — HCM
Detailed Module Breakdown
1. Finance (FI)

Description:
SAP FI (Financial Accounting) manages the company's financial accounting and reporting activities. It helps organizations maintain accurate financial records and monitor their overall financial position.

Key Functions:

Handles accounting and financial transactions.
Manages accounts payable and accounts receivable.
Maintains general ledger records.
Supports financial reporting and real-time financial closing.
Tracks income, expenses, assets, and liabilities.

Use Case:
For example, a company can use SAP FI to record daily sales revenue, manage customer payments, track expenses, and prepare financial statements.

2. Controlling (CO)

Description:
SAP CO (Controlling) focuses on internal cost management and profitability analysis. It helps management understand where money is being spent and how profitable different business activities are.

Key Functions:

Works closely with FI as part of SAP FICO.
Monitors and controls internal costs.
Tracks costs related to departments, projects, and internal orders.
Measures profitability by product, project, or business segment.
Supports planning and budgeting activities.

Use Case:
A company can use SAP CO to compare the actual cost of a project with its planned budget and determine whether the project is profitable.

3. Material Management (MM)

Description:
SAP MM (Materials Management) manages procurement, purchasing, inventory, and material-related processes within an organization.

Key Functions:

Creates and manages purchase requisitions and purchase orders.
Handles goods receipts and material movements.
Manages supplier information.
Supports invoice verification.
Tracks and optimizes inventory and stock levels.
Helps maintain proper availability of raw materials.

Use Case:
A manufacturing company can use SAP MM to automate purchasing processes, manage suppliers, and track raw material inventory from procurement to production.

4. Production Planning (PP)

Description:
SAP PP (Production Planning) manages the planning and execution of manufacturing activities. It helps companies produce the required quantity of products at the right time.

Key Functions:

Handles production scheduling.
Supports capacity planning.
Performs Material Requirements Planning (MRP).
Manages production orders.
Monitors production activities in real time.
Integrates with MM for materials and SD for customer requirements.

Use Case:
For example, an electronics manufacturer can use SAP PP to plan production according to customer demand, ensure sufficient raw materials are available, and optimize production schedules during seasonal demand spikes.

5. Human Capital Management (HCM)

Description:
SAP HCM (Human Capital Management) manages an organization's employee-related and human resource processes.

Key Functions:

Maintains employee information and personnel records.
Manages payroll processing.
Handles employee attendance and leave.
Supports employee self-service.
Manages performance appraisals and employee development.
Integrates with Finance for payroll-related accounting.

Use Case:
An organization can use SAP HCM to maintain employee records, process monthly salaries, manage leave and attendance, and conduct employee performance evaluations.

6. Sales & Distribution (SD)

Description:
SAP SD (Sales and Distribution) manages the complete sales process, starting from customer inquiries and quotations through sales orders, delivery, and billing.

Key Functions:

Manages customer and sales information.
Handles inquiries, quotations, and sales orders.
Manages product pricing, discounts, and taxes.
Supports delivery and shipping processes.
Handles billing and invoice generation.
Integrates closely with MM and FI/CO.

Use Case:
For example, when a customer places an order, SAP SD can create the sales order, check product availability, arrange delivery, generate the invoice, and transfer the financial information to FI.

Overall SAP Integration

These modules work together to provide an integrated business environment. For example:

Customer Order (SD) → Material Availability (MM) → Production (PP) → Delivery & Billing (SD) → Financial Posting (FI) → Cost & Profit Analysis (CO)

This integration allows companies to reduce manual work, improve data accuracy, monitor business activities in real time, and make better management decisions.

